WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.696
NARRATOR: We begin by distinguishing how
signals exist in time.

2
00:00:05.696 --> 00:00:14.013
NARRATOR: A continuous signal varies smoothly
without interruption, like the voltage across

3
00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:16.785
a capacitor charging gradually.

4
00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:23.719
NARRATOR: Mathematically, we describe it with
a function that accepts any real number for

5
00:00:23.719 --> 00:00:24.214
time.

6
00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:32.392
NARRATOR: A discrete signal, however, only
exists at specific, separate moments.

7
00:00:32.392 --> 00:00:40.475
NARRATOR: Think of a metronome ticking or a
digital audio file recording sound only at

8
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:41.553
fixed intervals.

9
00:00:41.553 --> 00:00:48.985
NARRATOR: The key difference lies in the
domain: continuous signals use real-valued

10
00:00:48.985 --> 00:00:53.321
time, while discrete signals use integer
indices.

11
00:00:53.321 --> 00:01:01.173
NARRATOR: This distinction shapes how we
analyze, store, and process information in

12
00:01:01.173 --> 00:01:02.482
engineering systems.

13
00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:09.604
NARRATOR: Understanding this boundary is
essential before we model how systems respond

14
00:01:09.604 --> 00:01:12.571
to these different input types.

15
00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:17.761
NARRATOR: Next, we model how systems process
these signals.

16
00:01:17.761 --> 00:01:24.425
NARRATOR: A linear time-invariant system
behaves consistently regardless of when you

17
00:01:24.425 --> 00:01:26.243
apply an input.

18
00:01:26.243 --> 00:01:34.003
NARRATOR: Linearity means doubling the input
doubles the output, and time-invariance means

19
00:01:34.003 --> 00:01:38.529
the system's behavior doesn't shift over time.

20
00:01:38.529 --> 00:01:45.728
NARRATOR: We characterize such systems using
differential equations for continuous domains

21
00:01:45.728 --> 00:01:49.654
or difference equations for discrete domains.

22
00:01:49.654 --> 00:01:56.011
NARRATOR: These equations capture how the
system's internal state evolves.

23
00:01:56.011 --> 00:02:03.379
NARRATOR: By solving them, we predict output
behavior without simulating every possible

24
00:02:03.379 --> 00:02:03.993
input.

25
00:02:03.993 --> 00:02:11.897
NARRATOR: This mathematical framework allows
us to analyze complex circuits or

26
00:02:11.897 --> 00:02:18.363
communication channels using fundamental
building blocks rather than trial-and-error

27
00:02:18.363 --> 00:02:19.082
experimentation.

28
00:02:19.226 --> 00:02:27.215
NARRATOR: To fully understand an LTI system,
we examine its response to a single,

29
00:02:27.215 --> 00:02:30.068
instantaneous spike called an impulse.

30
00:02:30.068 --> 00:02:38.028
NARRATOR: This impulse response reveals
exactly how the system reacts to any arbitrary

31
00:02:38.028 --> 00:02:38.640
input.

32
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:46.675
NARRATOR: When we feed a general signal into
the system, the output emerges from combining

33
00:02:46.675 --> 00:02:50.961
the impulse response with the input through
convolution.

34
00:02:50.961 --> 00:02:56.942
NARRATOR: Convolution mathematically slides
the impulse response across the input, scales

35
00:02:56.942 --> 00:03:01.836
it, and sums the products at each time point.

36
00:03:01.836 --> 00:03:09.140
NARRATOR: This operation captures how past
inputs influence the current output.

37
00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:16.525
NARRATOR: By mastering convolution, we can
predict system behavior for complex waveforms

38
00:03:16.525 --> 00:03:23.909
without solving differential equations from
scratch, making it a cornerstone of signal

39
00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:25.140
processing analysis.

40
00:03:25.298 --> 00:03:32.301
NARRATOR: To analyze signals across
frequencies, we use complex exponentials.

41
00:03:32.301 --> 00:03:39.353
NARRATOR: These functions oscillate with
constant magnitude while rotating in the

42
00:03:39.353 --> 00:03:45.123
complex plane, making them ideal for
representing sinusoidal waves.

43
00:03:45.123 --> 00:03:54.111
NARRATOR: The Fourier representation
decomposes any signal into a sum of these

44
00:03:54.111 --> 00:03:58.605
complex exponentials, revealing its frequency
content.

45
00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:06.439
NARRATOR: Instead of working with time-domain
waveforms, we shift to the frequency domain

46
00:04:06.439 --> 00:04:10.658
where signals become vectors of complex
coefficients.

47
00:04:10.658 --> 00:04:17.594
NARRATOR: This transformation simplifies
system analysis because multiplication in the

48
00:04:17.594 --> 00:04:23.837
frequency domain corresponds to convolution in
the time domain.

49
00:04:23.837 --> 00:04:31.926
NARRATOR: Engineers use this view to design
filters and modulators by manipulating

50
00:04:31.926 --> 00:04:37.319
frequency components directly rather than
solving differential equations.

51
00:04:37.466 --> 00:04:45.002
NARRATOR: The frequency response describes how
an LTI system modifies different frequency

52
00:04:45.002 --> 00:04:45.630
components.

53
00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:53.094
NARRATOR: It shows both magnitude attenuation
and phase shift across the spectrum.

54
00:04:53.094 --> 00:05:00.330
NARRATOR: When a signal passes through a
system, each complex exponential component

55
00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:08.170
gets scaled and rotated according to the
system's frequency response at that specific

56
00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:08.773
frequency.

57
00:05:08.773 --> 00:05:16.778
NARRATOR: This means filters can selectively
amplify or suppress certain frequencies while

58
00:05:16.778 --> 00:05:18.112
preserving others.

59
00:05:18.112 --> 00:05:25.819
NARRATOR: By examining the magnitude plot, we
see which frequencies pass through unchanged,

60
00:05:25.819 --> 00:05:29.969
and the phase plot reveals timing delays.

61
00:05:29.969 --> 00:05:37.606
NARRATOR: Consistent predictions between time
and frequency domains confirm our model

62
00:05:37.606 --> 00:05:45.243
accurately captures the system's behavior,
allowing reliable design of audio equalizers

63
00:05:45.243 --> 00:05:47.326
or communication channels.

64
00:05:47.474 --> 00:05:55.396
NARRATOR: When we convert a continuous signal
to digital form, we sample it at regular

65
00:05:55.396 --> 00:05:55.924
intervals.

66
00:05:55.924 --> 00:06:03.368
NARRATOR: This process creates repeated copies
of the original spectrum, spaced by the

67
00:06:03.368 --> 00:06:04.513
sampling frequency.

68
00:06:04.513 --> 00:06:11.859
NARRATOR: Each copy represents the same
frequency content but shifted in the frequency

69
00:06:11.859 --> 00:06:12.424
domain.

70
00:06:12.424 --> 00:06:18.667
NARRATOR: If the original signal contains
frequencies higher than half the sampling

71
00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:23.870
rate, these shifted copies will overlap with
the base spectrum.

72
00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:30.917
NARRATOR: This overlap makes distinct
frequencies indistinguishable after sampling,

73
00:06:30.917 --> 00:06:34.049
a phenomenon called aliasing.

74
00:06:34.049 --> 00:06:42.019
NARRATOR: To prevent this, we must ensure the
signal is bandlimited and apply anti-aliasing

75
00:06:42.019 --> 00:06:43.727
filters before sampling.

76
00:06:43.727 --> 00:06:50.301
NARRATOR: Proper sampling preserves the
original frequency information without

77
00:06:50.301 --> 00:06:51.031
distortion.

78
00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:57.616
NARRATOR: Reconstructing a continuous signal
from discrete samples requires careful

79
00:06:57.616 --> 00:06:58.905
workflow planning.

80
00:06:58.905 --> 00:07:06.673
NARRATOR: First, we identify the signal's
maximum frequency to determine its bandwidth.

81
00:07:06.673 --> 00:07:15.607
NARRATOR: Next, we apply an analog low-pass
filter to remove frequencies above half the

82
00:07:15.607 --> 00:07:18.798
sampling rate, preventing spectral overlap.

83
00:07:18.798 --> 00:07:26.441
NARRATOR: We then select a sample rate with
sufficient margin to avoid aliasing entirely.

84
00:07:26.441 --> 00:07:34.026
NARRATOR: Finally, we verify that the
reconstruction process accurately recovers the

85
00:07:34.026 --> 00:07:35.405
original waveform.

86
00:07:35.405 --> 00:07:43.375
NARRATOR: Increasing the sample rate after
aliasing has occurred cannot repair the lost

87
00:07:43.375 --> 00:07:48.280
information, as the overlapping frequencies
are permanently mixed.

88
00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:55.892
NARRATOR: This systematic approach ensures
digital systems faithfully represent analog

89
00:07:55.892 --> 00:08:00.459
signals for further processing or storage.

90
00:08:00.602 --> 00:08:07.980
NARRATOR: Let's trace a single sinusoidal
signal through a practical filter and sampler.

91
00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:13.016
NARRATOR: The input is a pure tone at a
specific frequency.

92
00:08:13.016 --> 00:08:19.933
NARRATOR: The filter first shapes the
spectrum, attenuating unwanted frequencies

93
00:08:19.933 --> 00:08:23.391
while preserving the target tone.

94
00:08:23.391 --> 00:08:30.373
NARRATOR: The sampler then captures this
filtered signal at discrete time points.

95
00:08:30.373 --> 00:08:38.583
NARRATOR: If the tone's frequency falls within
the passband and below the Nyquist limit, the

96
00:08:38.583 --> 00:08:42.962
sampled values will accurately represent the
original wave.

97
00:08:42.962 --> 00:08:50.534
NARRATOR: We can verify this by reconstructing
the samples and comparing them to the input.

98
00:08:50.534 --> 00:08:58.947
NARRATOR: This end-to-end trace demonstrates
how filtering and sampling work together to

99
00:08:58.947 --> 00:09:03.855
preserve frequency information while enabling
digital processing.

100
00:09:03.855 --> 00:09:10.816
NARRATOR: Engineers use this principle to
design communication systems that transmit

101
00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:13.980
audio or data without distortion.

102
00:09:14.138 --> 00:09:22.534
NARRATOR: A fundamental principle in signals
and systems is that equivalent time- and

103
00:09:22.534 --> 00:09:26.409
frequency-domain views must produce consistent
predictions.

104
00:09:26.409 --> 00:09:33.079
NARRATOR: When we analyze a system using
differential equations in the time domain, we

105
00:09:33.079 --> 00:09:39.748
should get the same output as when we use
convolution with the impulse response.

106
00:09:39.748 --> 00:09:46.310
NARRATOR: Similarly, frequency-domain
multiplication should match time-domain

107
00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:47.248
results.

108
00:09:47.248 --> 00:09:55.451
NARRATOR: This consistency allows engineers to
switch between domains based on which is

109
00:09:55.451 --> 00:09:56.713
mathematically simpler.

110
00:09:56.713 --> 00:10:04.504
NARRATOR: For example, we might solve a
complex circuit using Laplace transforms in

111
00:10:04.504 --> 00:10:11.695
the frequency domain, then verify the results
by simulating the time-domain response.

112
00:10:11.695 --> 00:10:18.764
NARRATOR: This cross-validation ensures our
models accurately capture physical behavior

113
00:10:18.764 --> 00:10:23.713
and prevents design errors in real-world
applications.

114
00:10:23.858 --> 00:10:29.700
NARRATOR: To apply these concepts, we use a
practical analysis checklist.

115
00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:37.593
NARRATOR: First, identify whether the signal
is continuous or discrete and determine its

116
00:10:37.593 --> 00:10:38.200
bandwidth.

117
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:45.218
NARRATOR: Next, model the system as LTI and
obtain its impulse response.

118
00:10:45.218 --> 00:10:51.811
NARRATOR: Verify that the signal frequency
falls within the passband and below the

119
00:10:51.811 --> 00:10:52.825
Nyquist rate.

120
00:10:52.825 --> 00:11:00.655
NARRATOR: Apply anti-aliasing filtering if
necessary, then select a sample rate with

121
00:11:00.655 --> 00:11:01.308
margin.

122
00:11:01.308 --> 00:11:08.908
NARRATOR: Finally, trace the signal through
the filter and sampler, checking that

123
00:11:08.908 --> 00:11:12.075
reconstructed values match the original.

124
00:11:12.075 --> 00:11:20.498
NARRATOR: This checklist ensures systematic
design, prevents aliasing, and validates

125
00:11:20.498 --> 00:11:22.183
frequency preservation.

126
00:11:22.183 --> 00:11:30.011
NARRATOR: By following these steps, engineers
can confidently build reliable signal

127
00:11:30.011 --> 00:11:36.415
processing systems that meet performance
requirements across diverse applications.
